{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EogFaFAzgVZzKBUwCYysie","type":"tool_result","content":"1140\t\n1141\t\n1142\t## Pre-analysis for US-173 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\n1143\tOkay, I'll analyze the user story and prepare the technical brief. First, I need to understand the project's structure.\n1144\tHere is the technical brief for implementing the A/B testing user story:\n1145\t\n1146\t**Project:** Pelangi Website (Next.js)\n1147\t**User Story:** US-173: Implement A/B testing with Vercel Feature Flags and Edge Config\n1148\t\n1149\t**1. Python/Frappe Files to Modify:**\n1150\tThis is a Next.js/TypeScript project, not a Frappe/Python one. No Python files will be modified.\n1151\t\n1152\t**2. Next.js Files to Modify:**\n1153\t\n1154\t*   **`middleware.ts`**: (New file at `site/middleware.ts`) This is the core of the implementation. It will intercept requests for the `/book` page, evaluate the Vercel feature flag, and set a cookie with the assigned variant (e.g., `x-cta-variant: 'B'`).\n1155\t*   **`site/src/app/book/page.tsx`**: This page will read the variant from the cookie (or a header passed from middleware) and pass it as a prop to the relevant components.\n1156\t*   **`site/src/components/Hero.tsx`**: This component will be modified to conditionally render the CTA button text based on the `variant` prop received from `page.tsx`.\n1157\t*   **`site/src/components/Rooms.tsx` or `site/src/components/RoomsTabsClient.tsx`**: These components will be updated to conditionally render different room card layouts based on the `variant` prop.\n1158\t*   **`site/src/lib/flags.ts`**: (New file) To define the feature flag configuration, following the \"Flags as Code\" pattern.\n1159\t*   **`site/src/lib/analytics.ts` (or similar)**: The analytics service will be updated to read the variant and send it to GA4 as a custom dimension.\n1160\t\n1161\t**3. Implementation Approach:**\n1162\t\n1163\t1.  **Install Dependencies:** `npm install @vercel/edge-config @vercel/flags`\n1164\t2.  **Create Edge Config:** Set up a new Edge Config store in the Vercel dashboard and link it to the project.\n1165\t3.  **Define Flag:** Create a feature flag in `site/src/lib/flags.ts` for the CTA test (e.g., `ctaVariantFlag`). Define the variants: 'Book Now', 'Check Availability', 'Get Best Price Direct'.\n1166\t4.  **Middleware Logic:** In `middleware.ts`, use `@vercel/flags` to evaluate the `ctaVariantFlag` for the incoming request to `/book`. Store the result in a cookie. This ensures the variant is determined server-side before the page is rendered, preventing flicker.\n1167\t5.  **Conditional Rendering:** In `Hero.tsx` and `Rooms.tsx`/`RoomsTabsClient.tsx`, access the variant and use conditional logic (e.g., a switch statement or ternary operators) to render the correct UI.\n1168\t6.  **Analytics:** In your analytics tracking code (likely in a component like `AnalyticsProvider.tsx` or directly in `page.tsx`), read the variant from the cookie and send an event to GA4 with the variant as a custom dimension.\n1169\t\n1170\t**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n1171\t\n1172\t*   **Bot/Crawler Traffic:** The middleware should filter out known bot user-agents to avoid skewing A/B test results.\n1173\t*   **Cookie Consent:** Ensure that setting the A/B test variant cookie complies with your site's cookie policy and the user's consent status. The variant cookie is arguably essential for site functionality in this context, but this should be verified.\n1174\t*   **Caching:** Be mindful of how Vercel's caching and Next.js's static generation might interact with the feature flags. The use of middleware should bust the cache correctly, but this needs to be tested.\n1175\t*   **Cross-Page Consistency:** If the user navigates away from and back to the `/book` page within the same session, they should see the same variant. Using a session-level cookie is the correct approach here.\n1176\t*   **Development Environment:** Mock the Vercel flags/Edge Config environment locally for development and testing. You can use a simple object or a local JSON file to simulate the flag values.\n1177\t\n1178\t\n1179\t## Pre-analysis for US-173 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\n1180\tOkay, I'll analyze the user story and prepare the technical brief. First, I need to understand the project's structure.\n1181\tHere is the technical brief for implementing the A/B testing user story:\n1182\t\n1183\t**Project:** Pelangi Website (Next.js)\n1184\t**User Story:** US-173: Implement A/B testing with Vercel Feature Flags and Edge Config\n1185\t\n1186\t**1. Python/Frappe Files to Modify:**\n1187\tThis is a Next.js/TypeScript project, not a Frappe/Python one. No Python files will be modified.\n1188\t\n1189\t**2. Next.js Files to Modify:**\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0509890-c56d-430e-b350-61a2b3042ba1","uuid":"bd2751d1-c947-4b56-a5ce-038103dd2811","timestamp":"2026-04-14T06:30:35.978Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\progress.txt","content":"\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-173 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nOkay, I'll analyze the user story and prepare the technical brief. First, I need to understand the project's structure.\nHere is the technical brief for implementing the A/B testing user story:\n\n**Project:** Pelangi Website (Next.js)\n**User Story:** US-173: Implement A/B testing with Vercel Feature Flags and Edge Config\n\n**1. Python/Frappe Files to Modify:**\nThis is a Next.js/TypeScript project, not a Frappe/Python one. No Python files will be modified.\n\n**2. Next.js Files to Modify:**\n\n*   **`middleware.ts`**: (New file at `site/middleware.ts`) This is the core of the implementation. It will intercept requests for the `/book` page, evaluate the Vercel feature flag, and set a cookie with the assigned variant (e.g., `x-cta-variant: 'B'`).\n*   **`site/src/app/book/page.tsx`**: This page will read the variant from the cookie (or a header passed from middleware) and pass it as a prop to the relevant components.\n*   **`site/src/components/Hero.tsx`**: This component will be modified to conditionally render the CTA button text based on the `variant` prop received from `page.tsx`.\n*   **`site/src/components/Rooms.tsx` or `site/src/components/RoomsTabsClient.tsx`**: These components will be updated to conditionally render different room card layouts based on the `variant` prop.\n*   **`site/src/lib/flags.ts`**: (New file) To define the feature flag configuration, following the \"Flags as Code\" pattern.\n*   **`site/src/lib/analytics.ts` (or similar)**: The analytics service will be updated to read the variant and send it to GA4 as a custom dimension.\n\n**3. Implementation Approach:**\n\n1.  **Install Dependencies:** `npm install @vercel/edge-config @vercel/flags`\n2.  **Create Edge Config:** Set up a new Edge Config store in the Vercel dashboard and link it to the project.\n3.  **Define Flag:** Create a feature flag in `site/src/lib/flags.ts` for the CTA test (e.g., `ctaVariantFlag`). Define the variants: 'Book Now', 'Check Availability', 'Get Best Price Direct'.\n4.  **Middleware Logic:** In `middleware.ts`, use `@vercel/flags` to evaluate the `ctaVariantFlag` for the incoming request to `/book`. Store the result in a cookie. This ensures the variant is determined server-side before the page is rendered, preventing flicker.\n5.  **Conditional Rendering:** In `Hero.tsx` and `Rooms.tsx`/`RoomsTabsClient.tsx`, access the variant and use conditional logic (e.g., a switch statement or ternary operators) to render the correct UI.\n6.  **Analytics:** In your analytics tracking code (likely in a component like `AnalyticsProvider.tsx` or directly in `page.tsx`), read the variant from the cookie and send an event to GA4 with the variant as a custom dimension.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n\n*   **Bot/Crawler Traffic:** The middleware should filter out known bot user-agents to avoid skewing A/B test results.\n*   **Cookie Consent:** Ensure that setting the A/B test variant cookie complies with your site's cookie policy and the user's consent status. The variant cookie is arguably essential for site functionality in this context, but this should be verified.\n*   **Caching:** Be mindful of how Vercel's caching and Next.js's static generation might interact with the feature flags. The use of middleware should bust the cache correctly, but this needs to be tested.\n*   **Cross-Page Consistency:** If the user navigates away from and back to the `/book` page within the same session, they should see the same variant. Using a session-level cookie is the correct approach here.\n*   **Development Environment:** Mock the Vercel flags/Edge Config environment locally for development and testing. You can use a simple object or a local JSON file to simulate the flag values.\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-173 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nOkay, I'll analyze the user story and prepare the technical brief. First, I need to understand the project's structure.\nHere is the technical brief for implementing the A/B testing user story:\n\n**Project:** Pelangi Website (Next.js)\n**User Story:** US-173: Implement A/B testing with Vercel Feature Flags and Edge Config\n\n**1. Python/Frappe Files to Modify:**\nThis is a Next.js/TypeScript project, not a Frappe/Python one. No Python files will be modified.\n\n**2. Next.js Files to Modify:**","numLines":50,"startLine":1140,"totalLines":1326}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01Cyx8jA4fL6FT1dGgwSU1ux","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I can see the pre-analysis was done already in the progress.txt, which mentions the files to modify and the approach. The story was marked as oversized diff (12854 lines, limit 500), meaning the previous attempt tried to do too much.\n\nLet me check the current state of the codebase to see what exists:\n1. Check if middleware.ts exists\n2. Check if flags.ts exists\n3. Check if @vercel/edge-config and @vercel/flags are installed\n4. Look at the current structure of /book page\n\nThe key issue is that the diff was oversized. I need to scope this to the minimal viable version that satisfies acceptance criteria:\n1. Vercel Edge Config store created and connected (this is dashboard work, not code)\n2. Packages installed\n3. A/B flag defined for /book page CTA copy\n4. Flag evaluated in middleware\n5. GA4 custom dimension records variant\n6. Vercel Flags UI (dashboard work, not code)\n\nMy plan should focus on:\n1. Installing the packages\n2. Creating a minimal flags.ts\n3. Creating middleware.ts to evaluate flags\n4. Modifying the /book page to read and use the variant\n5. Setting up GA4 integration for the variant\n\nLet me check the current structure first.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1566,"cache_read_input_tokens":55969,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1566},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0509890-c56d-430e-b350-61a2b3042ba1","uuid":"3738ba04-9a2c-4c7a-b57b-1628e24f3a3f"}
